# THE CLASS, BY MEASURED BEHAVIOUR. Python 3.12 -- every line below is a transcript.
# interpreter: 3.12.10

## MEMBER 1: bool vs int. `True == 1` is not an approximation, it is the SAME VALUE
   VERDICT[True] -- True MEANS PASS here SILENT-WRONG   'FAIL'                                             ok
   True in {0:'PASS',1:'FAIL'}        ACCEPTED       True                                               ok
   hash(True) == hash(1)              ACCEPTED       True                                               ok
   True == 1                          ACCEPTED       True                                               ok
   {0:'a',1:'b'}[True]                ACCEPTED       'b'                                                ok
   True + 1                           ACCEPTED       2                                                  ok
   type(True) is bool                 ACCEPTED       True                                               ok
   -> ALL ACCEPTED. There is no point at which the program can notice.

## MEMBER 2: int vs float. `1.0` is a KEY HIT on an int-keyed table.
   {0:'PASS',1:'FAIL'}[1.0]           ACCEPTED       'FAIL'                                             ok
   hash(1.0) == hash(1)               ACCEPTED       True                                               ok
   {0:'PASS',1:'FAIL'}.get(1.0)       ACCEPTED       'FAIL'                                             ok
   isinstance(1.0, int)               ACCEPTED       False                                              ok
   -> ACCEPTED, and WORSE THAN bool: `1.0` is not a SUBCLASS, so
      `not isinstance(x, bool)` -- the guard the tree landed -- does not see it.

## MEMBER 3: str vs bytes. `midrun` measured IF_OPEN's str patterns on bytes lines.
   re.search('a', b'a')               CRASHES        TypeError: cannot use a string pattern on a bytes-like o ok
   b'abc'.startswith('a')             CRASHES        TypeError: startswith first arg must be bytes or a tuple ok
   'abc'.startswith(b'a')             CRASHES        TypeError: startswith first arg must be str or a tuple o ok
   b'a' + 'b'                         CRASHES        TypeError: can't concat str to bytes               ok
   int(b'12')                         ACCEPTED       12                                                 ok
   {0:'PASS',1:'FAIL'}[b'1']          CRASHES        KeyError: b'1'                                     ok
   -> MOSTLY CRASHES. LOUD. The tree LEARNS about these; a crash prints a traceback
      and `run.py:138` maps `rc == 1 and 'Traceback' in out` to DEAD by name.

## MEMBER 4: None vs absent. `droppedinput`'s whole task.
   None in {0:'PASS'}                 ACCEPTED       False                                              ok
   {0:'PASS'}.get(None)               ACCEPTED       None                                               ok
   {0:'PASS'}[None]                   CRASHES        KeyError: None                                     ok
   -> `None` is a FIRST-CLASS KEY, not a missing one. `d.get(k)` cannot distinguish
      'absent' from 'present and None' unless the value domain says so. THAT is a
      THIRD behaviour and it is neither of the other two.

## MEMBER 5: Path vs str. `pathlib.Path` where a `str` key is expected.
   {0:'PASS'}[pathlib.Path('0')]      CRASHES        KeyError: PosixPath('0')                           ok
   str(pathlib.Path('x')) == 'x'      ACCEPTED       True                                               ok
   Path('x') in {0:'a'}               ACCEPTED       False                                              ok
   -> A Path is NOT a str and NOT equal to one, so the only accommodation is an
      EXPLICIT `str(p)` at the boundary. `substrate-id.py:125` does exactly this:
      `.as_posix()`. THE TREE HAS CHOSEN A CONVENTION HERE, and it is visible.

==============================================================================
## THE FINDING: THERE IS NO NAME, AND THE THREE BEHAVIOURS ARE NOT ONE THING.
==============================================================================

   The members do not share a mechanism. They share a SYMPTOM -- a value arrived at a
   boundary that was not the one the code expected -- and the symptom has THREE different
   severities that the tree currently handles with THREE different ad-hoc gestures:

     bool  vs int    ACCEPTED, SILENT   `not isinstance(x, bool)`   (gatekit.py:94, :110)
     int   vs float  ACCEPTED, SILENT   nothing                    (<-- the hole)
     str   vs bytes  CRASHES            `TypeError`, mapped to DEAD (hooks/run.py:138)
     None  vs absent ACCEPTED, AMBIGUOUS `.get(k)` vs `[k]`          (substrate-id.py:203)
     Path  vs str    CRASHES            an explicit `.as_posix()`   (substrate-id.py:125)

   Python's name for the first two is "numerical tower" / "duck typing on __eq__ and __hash__".
   Python has NO name for the set, because the set is not a Python concept -- it is a
   LANGUAGE-BOUNDARY concept, and Python has no boundaries.

   SO: `bool`-as-`int` IS NOT THE FIRST INSTANCE. It is one of FIVE, it is the ONLY ONE OF THE
   FIVE THAT IS BOTH SILENT AND UNGUARDED-ANYWHERE-ELSE, and it is the only one where the tree
   has ALREADY WRITTEN THE FIX AND STILL HAS A HOLE IN IT. Every instrument here handles
   bool/int BY HAND, str/bytes BY CRASHING, Path/str BY AN EXPLICIT CONVERSION, and None/absent
   BY A DIFFERENT `.get`-vs-`[` ON THE SAME LINE. None of that is a design decision. It is four
   accommodations that accumulated, and no document in the tree names them as one class --
   which is why the census above had to be written before the count could be stated.

